<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Appenzell Innerrhoden</h1><p class="st-meta">1597 CE – 1798 CE · capital Appenzell</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Catholic half of Appenzell, created by the canton's division in 1597, which still governs itself by an open-air assembly.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1597 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-appenzell">Canton of Appenzell</a>, divided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, was split in two with the mediation of the other Swiss cantons. The Catholic inner districts around the village of Appenzell formed Innerrhoden, keeping the old seat and archives.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Innerrhoden governed itself through its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a>, meeting in the square at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-appenzell">Appenzell</a>, which elected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landammann">Landammann</a> and passed laws. It shared a single vote in the federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Diet</a> with Ausserrhoden. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and dairy farming and later on embroidery done at home. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capuchins">Capuchin</a> friars strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The French invasion of 1798 merged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-appenzell">Appenzell</a> into the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canton-of-santis">Säntis</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. Restored in 1803, Innerrhoden still holds its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a>; it was the last canton to give women the vote, in 1990, after a federal court ruling.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/appenzell-innerrhoden.jpg" alt="Appenzell Innerrhoden" width="320" height="389"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHE_Appenzell_Innerrhoden_COA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1597 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Appenzell</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swiss German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Inner Rhodes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1698&amp;focus=appenzell-innerrhoden" aria-label="Appenzell Innerrhoden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4900 517 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Appenzell Innerrhoden lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5220 773L5220 773M5220 773L5186 782M5220 773L5188 772"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5220 773h0M5186 782h0M5188 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5220 773h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5220 773h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Appenzell. <a href="/atlas?year=1698&amp;focus=appenzell-innerrhoden">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
